# WifiDB PMTiles Archive Directory
# Apache configuration for the .pmtiles archives written by mvtd.php.
#
# Unlike out/tiles/, the files here are not fetched whole by a browser. They
# are read with HTTP Range requests — by pmtiles-swarm as a torrent web seed,
# and by any client using the pmtiles:// protocol. That makes three headers
# load-bearing that do not matter for ordinary tiles: Accept-Ranges, an ETag
# the client can actually read, and CORS.
#
# Deploy to: /srv/www/virtual/wifidb.net/out/pmtiles/.htaccess
# Requires:  a2enmod headers && systemctl reload apache2
#
# ── The one that will bite on the second server ──────────────────────────────
# Apache derives ETags from file metadata, and pmtiles.js throws EtagMismatch
# if the validator changes between range requests while reading one archive
# (js/src/index.ts). Two servers holding byte-identical archives produce
# different ETags unless every component of the validator is a function of the
# content, so a client whose requests are balanced across both fails part-way
# through a read.
#
# Inode differs between servers by definition. Mtime does too, once the
# archives arrive over BitTorrent: mtime is not in the metainfo and is not
# transmitted, so a delivered file is stamped with the moment its download
# finished. That was survivable while rsync -a did the copying, because rsync
# preserves mtimes; it is not survivable now, and no configuration of the
# mirror can make it so.
#
# So: size alone. It is the only metadata both nodes derive from the same
# bytes. The cost is that a rebuild producing an archive of exactly the same
# length is indistinguishable from the one it replaced, and a read spanning
# the swap would mix them silently. That needs the stable name, a same-size
# rebuild, and a read in flight across it — and the archives where a repeated
# size is actually likely are the few-hundred-byte ones, which are read in a
# single range request and so cannot be mixed.
#
# ── Putting mtime back ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# pmtiles-swarm 0.19.0 publishes the origin's mtime in the feed and restores it
# on the subscriber, which makes mtime a function of the build again rather
# than of when a node fetched it. That is what this workaround is waiting for.
# Three things have to be true before `FileETag MTime Size` can come back, and
# on 2026-08-14 none of them was:
#
#   1. Every node running 0.19.0 or later, and the archive BUILT since. Older
#      entries carry no origin mtime and are deliberately not backfilled, so
#      they keep diverging until their next build.
#   2. Nothing copying the archive without preserving timestamps. Both copy
#      paths were doing exactly that — tools/cron/link_archive now uses cp -p,
#      and mvt_publish_archive() now touches the stable name to match its
#      build. A hard link needs neither, being the same inode.
#   3. The check actually passing. Compare the two nodes:
#        stat -c '%Y %n' /srv/www/virtual/wifidb.net/out/pmtiles/*.pmtiles
#      Every archive present on both must show the same mtime. Measured on
#      2026-08-14: 0 of 20 agreed, by 59 seconds to 5.8 hours.

FileETag Size

# The directory listing would otherwise expose every retired build.
Options -Indexes

<IfModule mod_mime.c>
    # The MIME type recommended by the PMTiles v3 specification.
    AddType application/vnd.pmtiles .pmtiles
</IfModule>

<IfModule mod_headers.c>
    <FilesMatch "\.pmtiles$">
        # Byte ranges are the whole access pattern here. Apache serves them for
        # static files by default; this states it for anything that asks first.
        Header set Accept-Ranges "bytes" "expr=%{REQUEST_STATUS} =~ /^[23]\d\d$/"

        # A browser reading an archive cross-origin needs both of these. The
        # second is easy to miss and fails in a confusing way: without ETag
        # exposed, JavaScript cannot see the header at all, so pmtiles.js loses
        # its only means of noticing the archive changed underneath it and
        # silently mixes bytes from two builds.
        Header set Access-Control-Allow-Origin  "*"
        Header set Access-Control-Allow-Methods "GET, HEAD, OPTIONS"
        Header set Access-Control-Allow-Headers "Range, If-None-Match"
        Header set Access-Control-Expose-Headers "ETag, Content-Length, Content-Range, Accept-Ranges"

        # Deliberately short. The dated filenames are immutable and could be
        # cached for a year, but {bucket}.pmtiles is a stable name whose
        # contents change on every build. Revalidation is cheap — a 304 costs
        # one round trip — and serving a stale archive body against a fresh
        # directory is not a failure a client can detect.
        Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=300, must-revalidate" "expr=%{REQUEST_STATUS} =~ /^[23]\d\d$/"
    </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>

# Build state, not output. mvtd writes {bucket}.pmtiles.building and the
# writer's own scratch files alongside the finished archive, because an atomic
# rename requires the same filesystem. A half-written archive served to a
# client parses as a valid header pointing at bytes that are not there yet.
<FilesMatch "\.(building|tmp)$">
    <IfModule mod_authz_core.c>
        Require all denied
    </IfModule>
    <IfModule !mod_authz_core.c>
        Order allow,deny
        Deny from all
    </IfModule>
</FilesMatch>
